Skip next section Ghana says will apply to join BRICS with India's support 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 Ghana says will apply to join BRICS with India's support
Ghana's cabinet has approved a decision for the West African nation to formally apply to join the BRICS economic bloc , Foreign Minister Samuel Okudzeto Ablakwa said on Wednesday.&#160;
The announcement came during a visit by Indian Foreign Minister Subrahmanyam Jaishankar. India is one of the four original members of the bloc which has now grown&#160;to 11 nations.
Ablakwa said it had already sought India's support for its application.&#160;
"Joining India in BRICS will give us economic options, will diversify our base and our partnerships, and will also allow us to have more cooperation within the global south framework," he said.
Meanwhile, Jaishankar said India would support Ghana's aspirations but the final decision would require consideration by all member nations.
BRICS was founded by Brazil, Russia, India, and China in 2009 as a forum for emerging economies.
Skip next section Cockroach party seek police permission for protest against election chief 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 Cockroach party seek police permission for protest against election chief
Political pressure group the&#160; Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) on Wednesday submitted a notice to the Delhi Police, formally informing them of the proposed protest on Saturday&#160;to demand the resignation of India's Chief Election Commissioner over allegations of bias and misconduct.&#160;
CJP co-convener Saurav Das visited the police station close to the Indian Parliament and addressed reporters after, saying:&#160;"We informed them that the 'Cockroach Janta Party' and the youth of the entire nation, all these 'cockroaches,' are set to gather at Jantar Mantar on October 10."
"We asked them to maintain law and order, cooperate with us and make necessary traffic arrangement," Das added, as quoted by the Times of India. &#160;
Jantar Mantar is an 18th century astronomical observatory in central Delhi, close to the Indian Parliament, Delhi Police Headquarters, Election Commission of India and other key buildings.
It is also a designated protest site which acted as the base of the recent youth protests in India which led to the ouster of Prime Minister Narendra Modi's former education minister.
Skip next section 'We will do it': PM Modi on India's development goal 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 'We will do it': PM Modi on India's development goal
Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi gave a rallying speech with a new slogan on Wednesday, at an event to mark the leader's 25 years as head of a government.
Speaking to an auditorium full of supporters in New Delhi, Modi expressed gratitude to the masses for keeping him in power for 25 years, claiming to have transformed India over the period.&#160;
"On 7 October 2001, when I took oath as the Chief Minister of Gujarat, I never imagined that this milestone would one day arrive," Modi said.
"Today, whatever I am, wherever I am, whatever responsibility I hold, the greatest contribution to it is that of the people — the masses. For me, the people — the masses — are the very form of God," he said.&#160;
Past the 18-minute mark of the speech which lasted for over an hour, Modi moved on from reminiscing about the past and outlined his hopes for the future.
Chants of 'jai shree Ram' — a slogan celebrating Hindu God Rama, which has turned political under Modi's party —&#160;rang in the background.&#160;
"The dream we are seeing today in 2026 of a developed India will surely come true. There is no reason for India to stop now. A hundred years ago, we were even less than 40 crore; today, we are more than 140 crore (1.4 billion). The 140 crore countrymen together will build a developed India by 2047, and we certainly will," Modi said.&#160;
"We will do it," he said in English, a rarity when Modi is addressing an Indian crowd.&#160;&#160;
Skip next section Lunch and a letter to India's election chief 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 Lunch and a letter to India's election chief
About 150-200 lawmakers from India's opposition are expected to convene at a restaurant in Delhi serving south Indian cuisine.
On invitation from Indian National Congress (INC) President Mallikarjun Kharge, the Members of Parliament (MPs) will be signing a letter requesting a meeting with the Chief Election Commissioner, news agency ANI reported.&#160;
This comes a day after Congress Leaders Rahul Gandhi, Priyanka Vadra, their supporters and over 200 other MPs marched towards the Election Commission of India (ECI) to demand the same meeting. Police eventually blocked the demonstrators near the ECI and detained several of them, only to release them later.&#160;
In response to the protest, the ECI said "no political party, through its representatives, had sought an appointment for a meeting with the Election Commission," as of 4:00 p.m. local time. It also pointed to specific procedures laid down in 2025 to meet with political parties.&#160;
It further called the proposed venue of the meeting, the Parliament House Annexe, "unusual" and said any such meeting&#160;would only be held at the premises of the ECI.&#160;
Dangerously close to lunch time, this reporter is now wondering what the&#160;lawmakers will order.&#160;
