Skip next section India's central bank raises policy rate to counter inflation 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 India's central bank raises policy rate to counter inflation
The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) raised the the key benchmark policy rate, for the first time in over three years, as it looked to control rising inflation amid the US-led war on Iran.
The repo rate, which guides the short-term lending rate for commercial banks, was raised by 25 basis points to 5.5%. The rate hike will make home loans, vehicle loans and corporate loans more expensive for borrowers. Even equated monthly installments would go up for existing borrowers.&#160;
With this, the RBI has shifted gears to join several central banks around the world looking to curb rising prices or boost their currencies. It has changed its stance to a "calibrated tightening," the report said.
Prices of basic food items in India have risen sharply over the Middle East crisis Image: Diptendu Dutta/NurPhoto/picture alliance
"Driven by escalating energy costs and rising food prices, global inflation is projected to increase sharply prompting monetary policy tightening by major central Banks," RBI Governor Sanjay Malhotra said in his opening remarks while announcing the rake hike.
He attributed the inflationary pressures to the "reescalation of the West Asia conflict in September and consequent hardening and volatility in global crude prices."
"Further tightening of global financial conditions, uncertainty about fair valuation of AI stocks, and an elusive resolution of the West Asia conflict pose significant downside risks to the global economic outlook," Malhotra said.
Nonetheless, the RBI governor said he expects India's "strong" economy to "remain resilient."
Apart from rising prices, the RBI's calculations likely involve the state of the&#160;Indian rupee which has been near record lows over the past week.&#160;
The central bank has rolled out a number of measures to attract dollar inflows to counter the rupee's fall but pressures from global investment outflows and crude prices persist.
Skip next section Rahul Gandhi, Priyanka Vadra booked by police a day after protests 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 Rahul Gandhi, Priyanka Vadra booked by police a day after protests
Police authorities in Delhi have registered a First Information Report (FIR) against siblings Rahul Gandhi and Priyanka Vadra, along with their supporters, for allegedly forcibly entering the headquarters of All India Radio during the opposition's march against Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar. The move was reported by news agency Press Trust of India.
Gandhi and Vadra are lawmakers and among Modi's biggest critics in the parliament.&#160;
As per the report, police received a complaint that the leaders, their supporters and other members of parliament entered the premises of the Akashvani Bhawan building without permission.
They did so after they were stopped near the Election Commission Office, continuing their protest as a sit-in&#160;to demand a meeting with the electoral body.&#160;
The Gandhi siblings allegedly forced their way into the building Image: Sanchit Khanna/Hindustan Times/Sipa USA/picture alliance
