Skip next section BJP marks 25 years of Modi as head of a government 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 BJP marks 25 years of Modi as head of a government
Seva Sankalp roughly translates to 'service pledge' or a 'pledge to serve' Image: MANJUNATH KIRAN/AFP/Getty Images India's ruling Bharatiya Janata Party (BJP)&#160;launched the culmination of a celebratory campaign&#160;—&#160; dubbed 'Seva Sankalp' — to mark Prime Minister Narendra Modi 's 25 years as head of a government.&#160;
On October 7, 2001, Modi was first sworn in as chief minister of the western state of Gujarat.&#160;
"As the head of the government, Prime Minister Shri Narendra Modi's twenty-five years are a testament to service, dedication, good governance, and strong willpower," India's Defense Minister Rajnath Singh said in a post on X.&#160;
Several politicians from the BJP echoed his&#160;sentiments, contratulating the premier on his service.
At the center of today's festivities is a bike rally. News agency ANI reported that participants loaded up on bikes arrived in the northern holy city of Varanasi from a different holy city of Tirupati in southern India.
"Covering 2000 km (1,242 miles) after visiting the Balaji temple in Andhra Pradesh, a team of 40 youths—who are also party office-bearers—undertook a journey," a local minister from the state of Uttar Pradesh said, adding that a temple would soon be built to mark the occasion.&#160;
Several states BJP-ruled states are organizing public gatherings where speeches by Modi and BJP party President Nitin Nabin would be telecast on giant screens.&#160;
On Tuesday, school students in Modi's home state of Gujarat made colorful formations as part of the campaign Image: ANI News/IMAGO
The celebrations are reminiscent of another recent campaign for Modi's birthday on September 17. Critics had then pointed out that the government was spending massively&#160;on public relations, advertising and celebrations, while advising Indians to practice austerity to counter the fallout from global conflicts.
Skip next section Tight security in central Delhi after Gandhi's protest 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 Tight security in central Delhi after Gandhi's protest
Visuals coming out of central Delhi on Wednesday morning showed significant police presence, barricades and diverted routes, as authorities stood alert for another round of protesters.&#160;
This comes less than a day after leaders of India's opposition bloc marched on the Election Commission of India to demand a meeting with India's election body over allegations of 'vote theft.'
The march soon turned into a sit-in which ultimately culminated in police detaining several leaders including opposition leader Rahul Gandhi and his sister and lawmaker Priyanka Vadra. They were later released.
This dramatic image made the rounds on Indian social media after police briefly detained opposition leader Rahul Gandhi Image: AP Photo/picture alliance
"We only want to go ​to the election commissioners, ask them questions, and tell them you have stolen votes," Gandhi said.
On Wednesday morning, authorities once again issued a prohibitory order under Indian law which forbids&#160;sloganeering, speeches and public assemblies. It is a regular move to dissuade protesters from gathering and arms the police to detain demonstrators for disturbing public peace.&#160;&#160;
Skip next section Welcome to our coverage! 10/07/2026 October 7, 2026 Welcome to our coverage!
Good morning and welcome to DW's India news blog.&#160;
This is Mahima Kapoor reporting from Studio Delhi and I will be your guide through the headlines of the day.&#160;
It's the morning after leaders from India's oppotion bloc marched on the Election Commission of India over "vote theft"&#160;allegations.
Arguably Modi's biggest political contender, Rahul Gandhi, has vowed retribution against "traitors"&#160;who he claims have colluded to errode India's elecotral roll during the controversial revision exercise carried out under Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar .
This might sound like deja vu to anyone reading this blog regularly, but police in the national capital has beefed up security around central Delhi on Wednesday morning, in anticipation of more protests.
The demonstrations and allegations will likely cast a shadow on what is a milestone day for Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi. The leader's party is celebrating his 25 years as the head of a government with tweets, temple visits and at least one bike rally.&#160;

Expansion was a key issue at 'Berlin Process' talks in candidate country Montenegro this month seeking to further Western Balkan integration into the EU Image: Regierung von Montenegro Advertisement
The European Commission on Tuesday published a document called "Preparing for a wider Union," which sought to lay out new proposals on "the policy orientations and actions needed to prepare the [European] Union for future enlargements ."&#160;
Commission President Ursula von der Leyen said that "Europe's future depends on the choices we make today," as she tries to encourage progress at the EU's leaders' summit this month and in other upcoming talks on the matter.&#160;
The EU's executive proposed an array of changes — including a gradual integration process for would-be members, new safeguards "to ensure that commitments undertaken during accession negotiations continue to be fulfiled steadily after accession," and changes to voting rules.
They were trying to strike a balance between simplifying the accession process, incentivizing candidate countries to continue to pursue it, and allaying concerns among existing members about potential risks or costs of expansion.&#160;
Ukraine is the newest EU candidate country, seeking to accelerate the process amid Russia's invasion Image: Valentyn Ogirenko/REUTERS
In the quarter century after the end of the Cold War, the EU rapidly grew from a union of 12 member states to 28, before dipping back down to 27 when the UK left following 2016's Brexit referendum.&#160;
But the process has&#160;ground to a halt ever since, with a series of candidate countries stuck in lengthy negotiations and little apparent appetite among members to continue to expand.&#160;
In recent years, amid Russia's invasion of Ukraine and signs of the US vying for increased influence in parts of Europe, theoretical interest in expansion has picked up once more . But realizing the idea, with strict rules governing the accession process and the need for unanimous support among existing members for many core steps, has remained challenging.&#160;
EU enlargement: Montenegro on track to join bloc by 2028
